Source 1

URL	https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/managing-diabetes/artificial-pancreas
Site Author	National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK)
Abstract	
The National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases explains artificial pancreas technology, also known as automated insulin delivery. These systems connect a continuous glucose monitor, an insulin pump, and computer software that uses glucose information to adjust insulin delivery. The article describes different system types, including technology that can pause insulin when glucose is predicted to become too low and systems that can increase or decrease insulin delivery. It also explains that some systems still require users to enter meal information and that device choices depend on individual needs, age, training, and access. This source is valuable for understanding how connected devices can automate parts of type 1 diabetes care.

Source 2

URL	https://diabetesjournals.org/care/article/49/Supplement_1/S150/163922/7-Diabetes-Technology-in-Diabetes
Site Author	American Diabetes Association (ADA)
Abstract	
The American Diabetes Association's 2026 Standards of Care section reviews current diabetes technologies, including insulin pumps and automated insulin delivery systems. It explains that automated systems combine glucose sensor information with an insulin pump and an algorithm that adjusts insulin delivery. The guidance discusses clinical recommendations, device features, and the importance of selecting technology according to a person's circumstances, preferences, and ability to use it safely. It also notes that these systems may improve glucose outcomes and reduce time spent with low glucose. This professional source is especially useful because it summarizes current evidence and recommendations rather than focusing on one manufacturer's product.
